#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad8472-0eaf-547f-a51d-d9fd88b94c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" :question/math-academy-id "q-340690" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae0bf8f-009f-5cd9-a958-5250470d215a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342764" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9bd8424-cd28-568d-ba73-3aa7ea472675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095479} {:answer/id #uuid "38abde75-2551-5055-a50e-d986efdfd2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4x + 3)/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095480} {:answer/id #uuid "3c031738-7346-5134-8117-9aee7a7c7253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(2\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095481} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee719f1-7581-568a-8881-2ef944019149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((4x + 3))/(\\sqrt[3]{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095482} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef48c55-c62b-518e-a659-3edcd2ea1e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ((8x + 6))/(\\sqrt{(2x^{2} + 3x)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd41183-7068-5455-b0e0-8cdceb8831b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095478}] :question/id #uuid "2da730c2-fcbd-535d-85d1-2014785f31eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48850" :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(x)$ for $f(x) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3x}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5aa34c-8035-515e-9a22-0910ed15b1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336452} {:answer/id #uuid "8dec9f04-7eaf-5206-a8d3-7559c929a68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337312} {:answer/id #uuid "be24eea7-2334-5d10-9f49-6df8807cf6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337721} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e1b6c9-7b2d-5b89-b492-99eacd8fa4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337907} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4036bb-195b-5a9c-b701-d3dee3bfbc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338239} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b56870-5595-52b0-ae23-7f96f4a87f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "217bdf0c-2bc6-585f-ac52-fad4aec9092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4}{)}^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7{)}^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
